{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SJxpJrwpwe6fKskweMJX2d","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully to determine what tasks to propose.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a spec-driven regeneration experiment for Unix utilities in Go, verified via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: \n- `cmd/` - one package per utility\n- `pkg/testutils/` - differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - output formatting\n\n\n- `pkg/hashutil/` - hash/checksum shared code\n- `pkg/sizeparse/` - size parsing\n- `pkg/encutil/` - encoding utilities\n- `magefiles/` - build automation (external, do not modify)\n- `docs/` - specifications (human-authored, do not modify)\n\n**Current State**: I need to find which requirements are \"ready\" and need tasks proposed. Looking at the code status, I can see which use cases haven't been started yet so I can identify what work needs to be done.\n\nThere's a long list of unstarted use cases across multiple releases, ranging from format utilities and test infrastructure to command implementations like cat, echo, sort, and various other utilities that need to be built out.\n\nNow I'm checking the requirement_states to see which specific requirements are marked as ready, and I'm noticing that several SRDs have all their requirements in ready status—srd004-ts and srd062-touch are fully ready, and there are others I'm still examining.\n\nFor srd063-timeout, I see R2.1 through R4.4 are ready, but R1.1 through R1.4 aren't listed in the current states. Looking at the recent commit history, I can see that R1.1-R1.4 for timeout were just completed in a previous task, which explains why they're not showing up here.\n\nThe same pattern holds for srd064-shuf—R1.1-R1.4 are already done, and R2.1-R4.4 are ready. Now I need to figure out which release to prioritize. Looking at the code status across releases, rel00.0 is only partially implemented with some utilities still missing, rel01.1 hasn't started yet, and rel01.2 appears to be fully implemented.\n\nBut the requirement states only track specific SRDs with ready requirements, and the earliest ones with available work are srd004-ts from rel15.0, srd062-touch from rel08.0, and srd063-timeout and srd064-shuf also from rel08.0, along with srd065-factor. I'm seeing that many utilities from earlier releases like cat, nproc, echo, and others aren't showing up in the requirement states, which suggests their requirements might already be completed or tracked separately. Looking at the code status though, a lot of these same utilities show as \"not started,\" so there's some inconsistency I need to figure out about what's actually been done versus what still needs work.\n\nThe requirement_states list seems to be the authoritative source for what's ready to work on right now—it's specifically tracking R-items with \"ready\" status. So I should focus on the SRDs that appear there and work through them in release order, starting with rel08.0 which has srd062 through srd066, then moving into rel08.1 and rel09.0 as those are the earliest releases with ready requirements. Looking at the rel08.0 utilities, I can see which ones have all their requirements ready to go. The touch utility has a complete set of 16 requirements, while timeout and shuf each have 12 ready requirements with the earlier ones already completed. Factor and expr also have 12 ready requirements each in this release. I'm realizing I can't access the requirements.yaml file to get the weight information, which creates a problem since I need those weights to plan tasks properly. Without being able to read files or use tools, I'll have to work with what I can infer from the requirement states and assume uniform weights across the requirements.\n\nLooking at the commit history, there's a clear pattern where each task